{
  "summary": "Replaces the CAmount type alias for int64_t with a dedicated class type requiring explicit initialization and construction. Arithmetic operations on amounts are restricted to domain-appropriate operations, implicit conversions from booleans or floats are banned, and a sats unit operator is introduced for literals. Call sites and tests across consensus, mempool, wallet, GUI, and IPC are updated to conform to the new type.",
  "problem": "Because CAmount is a raw integer typedef, the compiler permits uninitialized amount variables, implicit conversions from boolean and floating-point types, comparisons across mismatched integer widths, and nonsensical operations such as multiplying two monetary values together.",
  "evidence": [
    "The PR author notes this prevents bugs previously fixed in commit 0026b330c4abbbbdb96e4f0c4d380d70d8e592ab (assigning from bool) and commit 890a09b1e49925315a5636f29cc5af36928fe092 (mismatched integral comparisons, which fail to compile if reverted on top of this PR).",
    "References prior attempts in #4067 and #5430."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": [
      "A future constrained amount type restricted to the valid range [0, MAX_MONEY]"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Very large refactor across 194 files; several commits are mechanical scripted diffs converting uninitialized declarations, explicit constructors, and literals with the `sats` operator.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Rebased with minor diff updates across test and utility files while maintaining the 12-commit structure.",
  "card": "This draft pull request turns CAmount from a plain int64_t typedef into a class type requiring explicit construction and initialization. The change enforces compile-time type safety against uninitialized monetary amounts, implicit conversions from booleans or floats, and invalid operations like multiplying two amounts together. As evidence, the author points to historical bugs (such as 0026b330c4 and 890a09b1e4) that now produce compile errors instead of runtime errors. The refactor touches 194 files across consensus, mempool, and wallet components, largely via scripted diffs.",
